<<<
1. Both ... as well as. To follow b. by as well as instead of and, as is often done either by inadvertence or in pursuit of the unusual, is absurd;
===
**1. *Both* . . . *as well as*.** To follow *b.* by *as well as* instead of *and*, as is often done either by inadvertence or in pursuit of the unusual, is absurd;
>>>

<<<
how absurd is realized only when it is remembered that the as well of as well as is itself the demonstrative to which the second as is relative, & can stand in the place occupied by both instead of next door to as.
===
how absurd is realized only when it is remembered that the *as well* of *as well as* is itself the demonstrative to which the second *as* is relative, & can stand in the place occupied by *both* instead of next door to *as*.
>>>

<<<
In the following examples, either omit both or read and for as well as; as teeli, it will be seen, can be shifted into the place of both, if the object is to give timely notice that the carters' strike, the composer, or the adjective, is not the whole of the matter:—He has figured prominently 6. in the carters' strike, in which man of his members were involved, as we as in the more recent railway strike. /
===
In the following examples, either omit *both* or read *and* for *as well as*; *as well*, it will be seen, can be shifted into the place of *both*, if the object is to give timely notice that the carters' strike, the composer, or the adjective, is not the whole of the matter:—*He has figured prominently b. in the carters' strike, in which many of his members were involved, as well as in the more recent railway strike.*/
>>>

